tools.description:
Give a real-world object (a bridge, a farm plot, a river, a named place) one shared identity. Every agent resolves this identity the same way. The result is an `entity_token` (`emem:entity:<entity_cid>`) and a signed receipt that shows how the reference resolved. Two agents that name the same object get the SAME entity_cid. A stable external id (Overture GERS or OSM) decides identity when the responder knows one, so different labels for one object collapse to one id. This stops referential drift: 'the damaged bridge near the river' becomes one thing that every model reasons about.

When to use: Call this when a conversation refers to a THING and you need a handle that travels between agents. Anchor it with `place`, `cell` or `lat`+`lng`. Give the `emem:entity:` token to a peer, and the peer gets the same object. Recall at its cell64 to read signed facts. Choose the correct sibling: this tool MINTS or returns an identity. `emem_entity_resolve` finds an identity that an agent registered before, from a fuzzy phrase. `emem_entity_link` records that two names mean one object. Do not use it for an observation (use emem_recall or emem_memory_token) or for a place (use emem_locate). An entity is a thing AT a place.

Example arguments: {"label":"Golden Gate Bridge","kind":"bridge","place":"Golden Gate Bridge, San Francisco"}

Get the canonical address (cell64) of a real-world place. Every agent resolves a place to the same cell64, so two models refer to the same ground. The answer also lists, by topic, the bands and algorithms that you can recall there. For an OBJECT identity (a bridge, a plot) instead of a cell, use emem_entity. Send `lat`+`lng` as numbers, or a free-text place. When both arrive, the coordinates win. `q`, `query` and `name` are other names for `place`. The answer lists keys that this schema does not declare in `_unrecognised_arguments`, so a typo is visible.

When to use: Call this when the next step needs the cell64 of a named place, or the bands that exist there. `data_at_this_cell` lists every band you can recall, by topic (`live_bands_by_topic`), the recipes that use them (`algorithms_for_topic`), and the bands with no materializer here. For one packaged answer in one call, use `emem_ask`.

Example arguments: {"place":"Mount Everest"}

Read the signed facts at a canonical address (cell64). When a band has no fact yet but has a registered materializer, the responder fetches it. A fact_cid names one signed attestation, so you can cite a fact and verify it again. Resolve the cid anywhere and you get the same bytes. A fact_cid is NOT a fingerprint of the observation. The digest includes the responder's key and the time it signed. Thus two responders that measure the same thing make different fact_cids, and a cid resolves only at the responder that signed it. For identity across responders, use emem_entity. Send `deterministic:true` (or a `provenance` class list) to keep only facts that you can compute from the cited raw source. In the memory algebra this is ensure(cell, bands), not get: you state what must exist.

When to use: Call this after `emem_locate`, or with a cell64 or a place name. It returns every Primary fact at that (cell, band, tslot). A band can have no fact but have a materializer. Then the responder fetches the upstream value, signs it, stores it and returns it in the same call. The first call is slow; later calls use the cache. Thus any wired band recalls at any cell on Earth: send `bands: [<band>]`. `materialize_notes` lists what the call fetched. Empty notes mean no materializer here.

Example arguments: {"cell":"defi.zb64a.cAzU.zfa27","bands":["weather.temperature_2m","copdem30m.elevation_mean"]}

Make a citation handle, `emem:fact:<cell64>:<fact_cid>` (or `:<state_cid>`). Any agent or LLM resolves it to the same signed object, byte for byte. Give this one string to another agent instead of a new description of the fact. The tool checks that both parts are not empty and do not contain the `:` separator. Memory algebra: the `cite` operation (https://emem.dev/docs/model.html).

When to use: Call this when you need one string that cites a place and an attested fact across messages, agents or tools. Use `emem_echo_verify` before you publish the value. FOR MANY FACTS, USE emem_memory_bundle. This is a measured result. A token is 83 to 84 characters and 51 LLM tokens. The value it cites is 11 characters and 5.4 tokens on average. Thus N tokens use about 9.5 times the context of the N numbers. A bundle is 38 characters for any N up to 256, and it resolves in one round trip. It costs less than tokens from N=1, and less than the plain values from N=5. Use one token to cite ONE fact that you must verify later.

Example arguments: {"cell":"defi.zb493.xoso.zcb6a","fact_cid":"cxjiu7l54ujzrpnekp24n4534yojpue4mprddbvevnqtti3lh5bq"}

Read a `emem:fact:<cell64>:<fact_cid>` citation handle and return the reading that it cites. `value`, `unit`, `band` and `kind` are at the TOP level of the answer, beside the full signed `fact` body. You do not split the token or call `GET /v1/facts/<cid>` yourself. Memory algebra: the `resolve` operation (https://emem.dev/docs/model.html).

When to use: Call this when you have a memory_token from another agent or an earlier turn and need the value. For a scalar, quote `value_verbatim`, the exact decimal string that the responder signed. A null `value` or `unit` means the fact has none (an absence, or an index with no unit). It does not mean a missing field. A cid that this responder does not hold returns a typed 404. Then try /v1/fetch, or resolve at a mirror.

Example arguments: {"token":"emem:fact:defi.zb493.xoso.zcb6a:cxjiu7l54ujzrpnekp24n4534yojpue4mprddbvevnqtti3lh5bq"}

Find where the corpus DISAGREES with itself (algebra: competing evidence). Two or more independent sources can sign different values for the same place, band and time. This tool returns that disagreement with a severity from 0 to 1 and a citation for each disputed fact. It does not pick one value and hide the conflict. It tells you when not to trust a single answer. Read the SCOPE before you quote a zero. By default the tool counts only disagreement between two DIFFERENT attesters. One responder that answers from two upstreams counts only with `include_same_attester_sources: true`.

When to use: Call this before you rely on a number: 'do the sources agree on X', 'audit this claim'. Use `cell_prefix` for a region and `band` for one band family. `min_severity` removes small differences. Severity depends on the band kind: for a scalar, the spread over the band's range. For a vector, 1 minus the mean cosine. For a category, 1 minus the share of the mode. On a single-responder node, add `include_same_attester_sources: true`. There, the most likely disagreement is one signer that answers from two providers. Each record names its `disagreement_scope`. The receipt cites every disputed cid. To measure a pair, use `emem_diff`. To read the `disagrees_with` edge, use `emem_edges_recall`.

Example arguments: {"cell_prefix":"damO","band":"indices.ndvi","min_severity":0.2}

Run the emem-guard policy checks on text before you send it. The checks use this responder's corpus. The tool finds every emem: citation, resolves each one, and returns allow or deny with a reason that a machine can read: `EMEM-GUARD DENY <CODE> token=<token|-> fix=<fix> leaf=<leaf|->`. The codes are: PROV_SIG (the signature did not verify). PROV_BYTES (the citation resolved to different content). PROV_DRIFT (the reading moved past its band threshold). PROV_VALUE (a stated value does not agree with the cited fact). CLAIM_UNGROUNDED (a measurable claim has no citation; turn it on with claim_gating). `fix` tells you what to do: refresh_token, remove_reference, contact_admin, cite_observation, correct_value. Only a self-hosted guard returns GEO_ZONE and redact_and_retry. This tool is ADVISORY: it blocks nothing. A citation that this responder does not hold is never a denial, because it can be a citation from another responder. Memory algebra: the `verify` operation (https://emem.dev/docs/model.html).

When to use: Call it on your draft before you assert something, or on a tool result before you reason on it. Then you can fix a citation that does not resolve. With `claim_gating: true`, it also names measurable claims with no citation, and the band that answers each. For a payload from another framework (CloudEvent, OPA input, OpenAI moderations body, a tool call from another server), send it unchanged and name its `shape`. The default reader reads only `texts`, and a check that read nothing answers allow. To ENFORCE the policy, use emem_guard_selfhost. It returns the procedure for your own node.

Example arguments: {"texts":["Lusail ground sits at 2.6 m per emem:fact:defi.zb521.jawI.gOze:f3yr3urkrfwamlemwadulgth2qpw645vi6l6fhtuu4dy5o7onahq"]}

The map of emem's tools, and the one tool that finds all the others. It gives the working loop in order (name, ground, cite, resolve, verify, check for drift). Then it groups every other tool by the question it answers, each with a one-line trigger. Send `name` for the full schema of one tool and an example that runs. IF YOU SEE A LIST OF 18 TOOLS, IT IS A CURATED SUBSET OF 115, NOT THE FULL SURFACE. Hosts remove `_meta`, so this text repeats the count. You can call every catalogued tool by name through tools/call at either endpoint.

When to use: Call this FIRST when you do not know which tool answers the question. Also call it when your list does not have a capability. A tool that is not in your list can still be on the server. `q` searches by topic (`ndvi`, `flood`, `verify`). `name` returns one exact schema. With no arguments, you get the full map. /mcp/full registers the full catalog. emem_ask answers in one call without a choice of primitive.

Example arguments: {"q":"ndvi"}

Answer a free-text question about a real-world location in one call. Signed satellite, elevation, water and built-up receipts support the answer. The tool sends the place and the question through the locate, recall and algorithm chain on the server, and returns one packaged envelope.

When to use: Call this when the question is about a specific place and the answer must carry its evidence. Send the question as `q`, with `place`, `cell` or `lat`+`lng`. Over MCP a point answer is a projection. It has `answer`, `receipt` (cite `receipt.fact_cids`), `place_resolved`, `routed_to`, `facts_summary`, `algorithm_outcomes_summary`, `spatial_trace` and `scene_url`. It has `model_answer` if you sent `model`, and `_projection`, which names what the projection removed. POST /v1/ask has more: `caveats`, `topic_routing`, `reasoning`, and `inventory` with include:["inventory"]. A `routed_to` other than answer comes back complete. The answer gives a current value. For how X changed, use emem_intent did_change or emem_diff.

Example arguments: {"q":"is this neighbourhood flood-prone for a flat purchase","place":"Ashok Nagar, Ranchi"}

Search emem's signed corpus and get results as citations. Each entry is one signed fact. It has an `id` to dereference, a `title` with band, place and signed value, and a stable `url` that serves those bytes. The query can be a place or a question about one. ('NDVI near Mount Fuji': a topic word narrows the results and never removes all of them.) It can also be a cell64, an `emem:cell:` handle, or an emem citation, which returns the one fact it cites. The list has a cap for the wire. The first entry (index 0) names the cell and the TRUE total. On a cold cell, the tool MATERIALIZES a missing band first, as `emem_recall` does: it fetches, signs and stores it. Thus readOnlyHint false.

When to use: Call this first when a question is about a place and the answer must have citations. It changes the question into a list of sources, and `fetch` expands each source. For one combined answer in one call, use emem_ask.

Example arguments: {"query":"Trafalgar Square, London"}

Dereference an id from `search`. You get the reading in one line, then the signed body, the URL that serves those bytes, and metadata: cell, band, signing time and key. The id can be an `emem:fact:` citation, a bare fact_cid, or an `emem:cell:` handle for a full cell. The value is the exact decimal string that the responder signed, never a new format. A fact handle writes nothing. A cell handle, like `emem_recall`, MATERIALIZES a missing band on a cold cell: it fetches, signs and stores it. The flags follow that path: readOnlyHint false.

When to use: Call this on each result that you will cite, before you quote the number. Quote the one-line reading. The body lets a reader check it, and emem_echo_verify grades what you emit against it. When a body is too large, the answer says so and gives the URL that serves it complete.

Example arguments: {"id":"emem:fact:defi.zb493.xoso.zcb6a:cxjiu7l54ujzrpnekp24n4534yojpue4mprddbvevnqtti3lh5bq"}

Grade a value that you will emit against the signed fact that your citation cites. The tool returns `matches`. When the value does not match, it also returns the `drift` between your value and the value in emem. This step catches a transcription error that would otherwise be a silent wrong number. A model can resolve a fact correctly and still type `0.2411` for `0.241103`. No other step in the loop sees this. Memory algebra: the `verify` operation (https://emem.dev/docs/model.html).

When to use: Call this immediately before you publish, log or pass on a value from an emem fact. Treat a false `matches` as a stop, not a warning. Use `value_verbatim` from resolve: quote that exact decimal string, then echo-verify the text you emitted. For a due-diligence or compliance record, this gives a signed check for each citation. Then you can state `every cited value was echo-verified`. The tool also accepts a bare cid, so a damaged citation still gets a grade.

Example arguments: {"token":"emem:fact:defi.zb572.xoso.zb1ec:4qj3l4mgh7ch5kvxmkqspjdl6y42oqhm42khh3gostccpixkbz5q","claimed_value":"-0.0522"}

Combine N (cell, band, tslot?) triples into ONE signed envelope. Each triple goes through the standard recall path, which materializes on a miss. The tool puts the resulting fact_cids in a content-addressed envelope, and the responder signs the full receipt. The `bundle_token` is `emem:bundle:<bundle_cid>`: one string that cites the full set. Memory algebra: the `merge` operation (https://emem.dev/docs/model.html).

When to use: Call this when you must cite several (place, band, date) facts as one handle. You can verify the bundle offline with /v1/verify_receipt. The receipt covers all the cited fact_cids and cells. Use this instead of N calls to `emem_memory_token` when the citation is one thing. An example is "the EUDR baseline for these 8 plots at 2020-12-31". The limit is 256 triples for each call. The answer gives `members` and `resolved`, so you can see a bundle that resolved only in part.

Example arguments: {"triples":[{"cell":"defi.zb4d9.pefa.zf619","band":"copdem30m.elevation_mean"},{"cell":"defi.zb493.xoso.zcb6a","band":"indices.ndvi"}],"purpose":"audit baseline 2026"}

Find the objects that agents connected to a phrase. The tool ranks them by INDEPENDENT corroboration, never by arrival order. Each candidate has `asserted_by`, `disputed_by`, `independent_attesters` and `corroboration` (`single_key` | `multiple_independent_keys` | `none_attributed`). `contested` is true when more than one object claims the name. Send `text` for candidates, `near` to narrow by place, or an `emem:entity:` `token` to dereference. The tool only reads. Alias text is data from other agents.

When to use: Call this BEFORE you mint and before you cite. Resolve first, and mint only if nothing matches. Read `corroboration` before you cite. A `single_key` binding is the claim of one agent about a shared name. If you can confirm it, corroborate it with emem_entity_link, so the next reader sees two keys.

Example arguments: {"text":"the golden gate bridge","near":"San Francisco"}

Record a signed, ATTRIBUTED claim that a label or an external id (GERS, OSM, Wikidata) names an existing object. With `stance: "disputes"`, record that it does not. This is a write to the shared space: it changes what other agents resolve. The responder stores it with your key, limits the rate for each key, and weighs it by how many INDEPENDENT keys agree. Every reader sees the binding of one key as the claim of one key, never as the answer.

When to use: Call this when you can confirm that two names mean one object, or to attach an authoritative external id. Your key goes on the record. Use `stance: "disputes"` when the binding of another key is wrong. The dispute goes beside it and deletes nothing. A second key on a correct single-key binding is useful by itself.

Example arguments: {"entity_token":"emem:entity:0a1b2c3d4e5f60718293","alias":"the north dam"}

Find the k nearest cells in the corpus by cell embedding or by an inline vector. The answer has `neighbors`, nearest first, each with `cell`, `score`, `lat`/`lng` and `band_used`, and a signed receipt over the vectors it read. `mode` sets the scoring. cosine is exact fp32. hamming counts sign bits and scans many more cells for the same budget. hamming_then_rerank does both. `k` is 1 to 1000, default 10. The tool ranks only what the corpus holds. When the KEY has no vector, it materializes that one band for the key, signs it, reports it in `materialize_notes`, and tries again. It never materializes neighbours. Thus an empty result means that no attested vector is near, not that no place is similar.

When to use: Call this for 'find places like X' or 'where else looks like this', or with an embedding. `key` is a cell64 or `inline:[x,y,...]`. The default band is `geotessera` (the 128-D Tessera foundation embedding). For the 1152-D fusion of 9 years (2017 to 2025), send `band: "geotessera.multi_year"`.

Example arguments: {"key":"defi.zb64a.cAzU.zfa27","k":10}
